Date: Tue, 30 Mar 93 09:29:46 PST
From: Robin Hanson <hanson@ptolemy.arc.nasa.gov>
Subject: PPL/MOVIE: "Unforgiven" Wins Academy Awards  

In case you hadn't heard, Clint Eastwood's "Unforgiven" just won 4
Academy Awards, including Best Picture, Director, and Supporting Actor.
I saw this movie last fall soon after starting to seriously think
about private law, and thought it was both one of the best movies I'd
seen in a few years, and found it somewhat inspirational re private law.

The plot is basic: A women doesn't get justice from the local state
law, and hires private enforcers to extract her revenge.  The movie is
about these enforcers doing their job; they are not condemned for
being "outlaws", but neither are they glorified.  They, and the movie
audience, have to directly face their choices and the full
consequences of their actions.  They choose their law, and then have
to live with it.  

This is one of the appealing aspects of the best of the Cowboy genre -
law and other social structures are not just given by God or history,
and are not obscured by layer upon layer of indirection.  The
characters must choose or build their society, and must personally
take on the various roles that implies - including judge, law-maker,
enforcer, butcher, warrior, trader, etc.

Robin Hanson

