Date: 30 Mar 93 15:21:28 EST
From: Duncan Frissell <76630.3577@CompuServe.COM>
Subject: LAW: offshore credit rating agencies

(Dale Worley)
 
>The retail clients are not savvy, and much advertising is needed to
>draw them in.  (And it does not pay them to be savvy.)  So offshore 
>banks do well with wholesale clients, but very badly with retail clients.

But the nets reduce transaction costs.  With a potential savings of 30% 
to 60% in taxes on interest, offshore investments have a great deal to 
offer even the smallest investors.  Also, Americans in particular have 
shown a willingness to shop around and experiment with alternative 
marketing forms in the search for small savings or small quality 
improvements in the purchase of physical goods.  The Mail Order 
revolution has had a big impact here.

We know that Americans do not comply with the revenue laws where the 
chances of getting caught are small.  75% don't withhold taxes from the 
wages of their domestic employees, 61% of expatriates don't file Federal 
Returns, 100% of leaseholders receiving rent payments from roommates do 
not report those receipts as income (to be offset against the rent 
expense).  As offshore investing becomes easier, they may do it 
directlty.  

In addition, those who *hold* the investments of small savers are sure to
take those asset pools and run them through the ringers of digital 
capitalism to produce all sorts of wonderful derivatives many of which 
can be profitably placed through offshore banks.   

>Credit bureaus are the same -- if you are dealing with a zillion small
>transactions, you pretty much have to stay within the law, because
>you've got to keep transaction costs low.  

It is the individual business that would consult an offshore credit 
reporting agency.  The laws controlling them are weaker.  The Fair 
Credit Reporting Act regs apply mostly to the credit reporting agencies 
themselves.  If an offshore agency existed, most businesses could 
consult it with little risk as long as they didn't mention their use of 
the info in doing any customer or employee turndowns.  The major problem 
with an offshore agency would be getting the credit info in the first 
place but the nets certainly speed up information exchange and reduce 
the costs of such sharing. 

