Date: Tue, 30 Mar 93 13:49:34 -0800
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: "Credit Oracles" and Freedom of Religious Practice

Duncan Frissell commenting on some objections to offshore credit rating
agencies:

>It is the individual business that would consult an offshore credit 
>reporting agency.  The laws controlling them are weaker.  The Fair 
>Credit Reporting Act regs apply mostly to the credit reporting agencies 
>themselves.  If an offshore agency existed, most businesses could 
>consult it with little risk as long as they didn't mention their use of 
>the info in doing any customer or employee turndowns.  The major problem 
>with an offshore agency would be getting the credit info in the first 
>place but the nets certainly speed up information exchange and reduce 
>the costs of such sharing. 

Indeed!

And there are some other interesting angles on this, too. Let me cite just
one, which may sound whimsical, but which also may show some doubter just
how hard it will be to ban the use of offshore credit rating agencies.

Suppose the Port Watson-based "Tim's Credit Agency" offers dial-up or
telnet service, perhaps through anonymous remailers. It offers the caller a
choice of "interfaces."

A Branch Davidian business based in Waco is considering a loan to Joe
Luser. They make a call to the Bahamas and ask for the "Revelations"
interface. The Revelations interface provides the Word of God to them:
"Thou shallt not lend to this man, for he hath defaulted on loans
throughout my domain, including a $25,000 business loan in Parsnippany, New
Jersey in 1978."

Who are the Branch Davidians to argue with God?

"Fair credit laws" can't interfere with the practice of religion, can they?
(There _was_ a case in which an avowed Satanist tried to get a job a Mormon
day care center. When she was refused the job, she sued. I never heard the
outcome.)

(I'm well aware of the fact that religious preferences often cannot be used
to exclude races, especially in business situations. That is, a Jewish deli
cannot have a sign "No blacks allowed," nor even, I suspect, "No women
allowed." Some religious groups are "allowed" to exclude on the basis of
sex (convents, etc.), and of course here in California, "men only" health
clubs are strictly verboten, while "women only" health clubs are
flourishing. Strange times we live in.)

I call this approach the "oracle end run." By invoking religion, new
lattitudes are allowed. A variant of the BD approach would be a
"confessional" in which "supplicants" ask a "priest" for advice, for a
small donation, about some business problem they're having.

Perhaps the clearest variant of all: consult "Madame Blavatsky's Psychic
Readings" for advice. Madame Blavatsky consults her crystal ball (hooked
directly to the data bases in the Bahamas, or even in cypherspace, in a few
years) and informs her customer of her findings: "If you lend to this man
you will have great grief."

And so on.

-Tim May

P.S. How likely is this oracle approach? Not very. Businesses will likely
find it easy enough to simply contact offshore agencies and ask for
"advice." Detective agencies (private investigators) can handle the
intermediary links, if needed. And the usual codewords, such as
"unreliable" can be used to signal a poor credit risk.

Only if the State steps in and demands that "proof" of unreliability be
demonstrated will the oracle end run have to be invoked. For the State to
then ban the use of divine guidance would have profound repercussions.


