Ah, here's my chance to do my Extropian deed for the day.

There ARE more than two choices here--it's not a matter of "my
experience happened and means just what I think" versus "your
experience is entirely fraud, conscious or otherwise".

I have nasty immune problems, including autoimmune reactions
that took a long time to beat back. Reacting to serotonin and
GABA, for example, can put a real dent in one's day. It can
also produce some very peculiar symptoms. While being tested
in allergy labs, I've seen myself go through bizarre mood
swings, have strange welts appear and disappear, feel various
kinds of pain for varying durations, suffered short-term
memory loss accompanied by spot zapping of long-term memories,
and lots of other stuff. Because it happened in reasonably
controlled conditions, I can be pretty confident that they
_were_ allergic reactions.

When I read COMMUNION, I found much of Whitley Streiber's
experience very familiar. It's just that there are whole
realms of possible cause that _both_ he and his critics
missed.

One can accept the accounts UFO abductees provide of the
experiences they consciously recall without giving credence to
the tales fabricated with the assistance of hypnosis and
without thinking of them as mere hysterics. But there are, in
at least some cases, explanations available within the world
as we already understand it to account for this stuff. And I'm
big on the principle of least hypotheses.


